data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"reasoning_content":"I need to inspect the rendered page. I can either install puppeteer temporarily or use a simpler approach. Since the task says optional peers shouldn't be needed, installing puppeteer might be acceptable for verification but shouldn't be in package.json. But I should avoid installing globally if possible.\n\nAlternative approach: Use the production build and serve it, then inspect the dist/index.html. The production build has the React app bundled. But still client-side rendered.\n\nActually, the simplest way to verify React client-side rendered output without a browser is to use React DOM server to render the App to static HTML. But that might not fully exercise the design system. However, I can do static rendering and check the data-component attributes.\n\nBetter yet, I can use a headless browser. Let me check if `chromium-browser` works with `--headless`. I can use it with a simple script. Or I can install puppeteer in a temp directory and use it.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: write a small Node script that uses puppeteer to visit the page, interact with buttons, and report DOM state. Install puppeteer in /tmp to avoid modifying the project.\n\nWait, but the instructions say \"Do not modify the application code; only verify and report.\" Installing puppeteer in /tmp is fine.\n\nLet me install puppeteer in /tmp and write a verification script."},"finish_reason":null,"index":0,"raw_output":null}],"created":1789959475,"id":"chatcmpl-751959713ec2493cbcbd7fd70d775fdc","model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k2p7-code","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null}

